 \section{Capabilities and Remaining Limits}\label{sec:sns_discussion}
 
-The AnimatLab attempt motivated a repeatable way to connect neural circuits to mechanical models and test their individual interfaces. The resulting tools provide converted geometry, a BPA-force interface, graphical neural components, controller ports, and automated comparisons. The reflex and rhythm-core tests demonstrate specific circuit behaviors; the bridge tests demonstrate closed-loop signal exchange. Mechanical import, controller transfer, and locomotion performance remain separate validation problems.
+The initial AnimatLab result showed suspended stepping-like motion but left its dependence on circuit organization, feedback, and mechanics unclear. The tools developed here make those dependencies testable in stages. Conversion repairs address whether forces act along the intended routes. Matched circuit tests address whether a translated implementation preserves selected neural responses. Coupled evaluations address how a controller behaves when its sensory input comes from the mechanical model. Keeping those questions separate prevents a successful component test from being treated as evidence for complete locomotion.
 
-The supported robot evaluations produced stepping-like motion and, in some configurations, forward progression, but no harness-free walking. Reducing tonic neural drive did not remove mechanical support. Spiking-neuron comparisons preserved a continuous motor-activation interface while changing rhythm timing and, in some runs, limb coordination (Appendix~\ref{app:simulation_verification}).
+The new results strengthen specific parts of this chain. The Rybak-derived neural model reproduced selected phase-resetting behaviors, a brief-pulse test produced self-sustained bursts in the Deng-derived circuit, and short-window comparisons checked network translation. The W2L tests connected pathway removals to bilateral contact cycling while exposing posture failures under perturbation. They also identify remaining problems: the Li transcription changes the neuron dynamics, the air-stepping mechanics do not yet follow the RG period, and applying previously unused parameter fields changed the W2L operating point and the interpretation of pathway removals. Its persistent cycling under mass changes did not satisfy the posture criterion in nine of ten conditions. None of these results establishes that the BPA-routed robot model reproduces the W2L result or that either model walks without a harness.
 
-The next step is to use these interfaces and tests to improve weight-bearing control and then evaluate transfer to hardware. Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework} discusses those extensions. 
+The resulting contribution is a set of neural, actuator, and mechanical interfaces with tests that distinguish implemented capabilities from unresolved model behavior. The next use of these tools is to compare controllers and feedback pathways under matched mechanical conditions, then evaluate whether the resulting behavior transfers to the robot. Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework} discusses hardware deployment and further extensions.
